WEBVTT

00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:07.580
<v Ira Glass>OK, so a couple of physicists walk into a bar. Just kidding. They're not at a bar. They're at a school. Scientists in training.
<v Ira Glass>好的，有两位物理学家走进了一家酒吧。开个玩笑。他们没在酒吧，而是在学校里。他们是未来的科学家。

00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:16.980
<v David Kestenbaum>I'm sure this story seems strange to you, but to me, it was just-- you know, this was just like another day in the physics world.
<v David Kestenbaum>我敢说这个故事听起来很奇怪，但对我来说，它仅仅是……你知道，这就像物理学界的寻常一天。

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:41.920
<v Ira Glass>David Kestenbaum used to live in physics world. These days he lives in our world. You can hear him in our world as part of NPR'S Planet Money team, reporting on economics. But at the time of the story, he was getting a PhD in high-energy particle physics at, um-- there's no way to avoid the name-droppy-ness of what I'm about to say-- at Harvard University. But to paraphrase Us Magazine , Harvard physicists, in certain ways around the office, they're just like us.
<v Ira Glass>大卫·肯斯滕鲍姆曾生活在物理学界。现在他生活在我们的世界里。你可以在我们的世界听到他，作为美国国家公共电台（NPR）《星球货币》团队的一员报道经济新闻。但在那个故事发生时，他正在哈佛大学攻读高能粒子物理学博士学位。嗯，我接下来要说的话难免有炫耀学校之嫌。不过，套用《美国周刊》的话来说，哈佛的物理学家们在某些方面和办公室里的人一样，他们也和我们没两样。

00:00:41.920 --> 00:01:00.960
<v David Kestenbaum>There was always a time of day when someone made a pot of very strong coffee, and afterwards, everyone drank the coffee and then didn't quite want to work yet. And so we all stood around and talked about various things. You know, and there was a blackboard or a whiteboard or something there. And we were talking about how nobody really had girlfriends.
<v David Kestenbaum>每天总有那么个时候，有人会煮上一壶非常浓的咖啡，之后，大家喝完咖啡，又不太想立刻干活。于是我们都站着聊天，谈论各种事情。你知道的，当时那里有个黑板或白板之类的东西。我们在谈论大家都还没女朋友的事。

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:12.520
<v Ira Glass>So, this being physics world, the next logical thing to do was to employ the power of mathematics to estimate the likelihood of finding a girlfriend. And so they start jotting down a calculation.
<v Ira Glass>既然是在物理学界，接下来合乎逻辑的做法就是运用数学力量来估算找到女朋友的概率。于是他们开始在板子上记下计算过程。

00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:16.060
<v David Kestenbaum>I guess it's sort of a variation on-- you know this thing called the Drake equation?
<v David Kestenbaum>我想这算是某种变体，你知道那个叫“德雷克方程”的东西吗？

00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:17.530
<v Ira Glass>No.
<v Ira Glass>不知道。

00:01:17.530 --> 00:01:24.540
<v David Kestenbaum>That is a way to estimate how many planets are out there that have intelligent life on them.
<v David Kestenbaum>那是一种估算宇宙中存在多少地外智慧生命行星的方法。

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:36.200
<v Ira Glass>OK, so in this Drake equation, apparently you start with how many stars are in the universe. That is, all the places where there might be life. And then you subtract out all the stars that don't have planets around them, right? Because there can't be life there.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，在这个德雷克方程里，显然你是从宇宙中有多少颗恒星开始计算的。也就是说，所有可能存在生命的地方。然后你要减去所有周围没有行星的恒星，对吧？因为那里不可能有生命。

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:47.670
<v Ira Glass>And then you subtract out all the planets that are too far from the sun or too close to the sun to support life. And so on and so on-- you get the idea-- until finally, you come up with the likelihood of a planet with life evolved to the point of intelligence. OK.
<v Ira Glass>接着你要减去所有距离太阳太远或太近而无法维持生命的行星。如此类推，以此类推——你懂的——直到最后，你得出了一个行星演化出智慧生命的概率。好吧。

00:01:47.670 --> 00:01:57.860
<v Ira Glass>They ran the same kind of math now, except-- and I realize this is going to sound a little strange. It says they replaced intelligent life with girlfriends.
<v Ira Glass>他们现在用了同样的数学方法，只不过——我知道这听起来有点奇怪。他们把“智慧生命”换成了“女朋友”。

00:01:57.860 --> 00:02:04.800
<v David Kestenbaum>So we started to do the calculation on the board. And, uh-- can you look up what the population in Boston is?
<v David Kestenbaum>所以我们开始在黑板上进行计算。还有……你能查一下波士顿的人口是多少吗？

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:23.050
<v Ira Glass>Now David is asking me to look this up because at this point in our interview, I actually made him run the math for me, with real numbers that we got from the internet. So he started with the population of Boston, because he and his fellow physics students wanted girlfriends in Boston, where they all live. Population of Boston, I found online, was a little under 600,000.
<v Ira Glass>大卫现在让我查这个，是因为在采访的这个时候，我确实让他用我们从网上得到的真实数据为我演示了计算过程。所以他从波士顿的人口开始，因为他和他的物理系同学想要在他们居住的波士顿找女朋友。我在网上查到波士顿的人口略低于60万。

00:02:23.050 --> 00:02:28.850
<v David Kestenbaum>So you start with 600,000, which sounds great, except that half of them are guys, right? And I'm only interested in girls.
<v David Kestenbaum>所以从60万开始，听起来不错，但其中一半是男性，对吧？而我只对女性感兴趣。

00:02:28.850 --> 00:02:30.550
<v Ira Glass>OK, so it's 300,000.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，那就是30万。

00:02:30.550 --> 00:02:36.640
<v David Kestenbaum>And then I want people-- let's be honest, probably within 10 years of my age or something, right?
<v David Kestenbaum>然后我想要那种……说实话，大概年龄在跟我相差10岁以内的吧？

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:39.320
<v Ira Glass>OK, so 10 years on either side, so that means--
<v Ira Glass>好的，前后10岁，那意味着——

00:02:39.320 --> 00:02:48.330
<v David Kestenbaum>I'm actually looking at some numbers here. Looks like-- looks like if you go from 20 to 40, you're talking-- that's still like 35% of the population, a third or something.
<v David Kestenbaum>我正在看一些数据。看起来……如果年龄范围在20到40岁之间，那大概是总人口的35%，也就是三分之一左右。

00:02:48.330 --> 00:03:05.990
<v Ira Glass>So that means that out of 300,000 women, that leaves 100,000 in his age range. These being doctoral students, they wanted girlfriends who were college grads. Well, OK, about 25% of Americans over 25 years old have graduated from college. That knocks out roughly 3/4 of these women.
<v Ira Glass>这意味着在30万女性中，他这个年龄段的还有10万。作为博士生，他们想要大学生女朋友。嗯，好吧，25岁以上的美国人中约有25%大学毕业。这剔除了这批女性中的大约四分之三。

00:03:05.990 --> 00:03:06.740
<v David Kestenbaum>Ouch.
<v David Kestenbaum>哎哟。

00:03:06.740 --> 00:03:11.500
<v Ira Glass>So you're down to-- we were at 100,000. So you're down to 25,000.
<v Ira Glass>所以剩下……我们刚才算到10万。所以你只剩下2.5万了。

00:03:11.500 --> 00:03:16.450
<v David Kestenbaum>Then you start applying stuff like, you know, how often are they single?
<v David Kestenbaum>然后你开始运用一些条件，比如，你知道，她们单身的概率是多少？

00:03:16.450 --> 00:03:21.840
<v Ira Glass>Yeah. Let's say half of them are single. So now you're down to 12,500.
<v Ira Glass>是的。假设她们中有一半是单身。现在你就只剩下1.25万人了。

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:24.250
<v David Kestenbaum>Yeah, see? It's getting scary now, right?
<v David Kestenbaum>你看，现在开始变得可怕了吧？

00:03:24.250 --> 00:03:37.090
<v Ira Glass>And then of course you get to how many people are actually attractive to you. And even if you give a really high percentage, like one in five, OK, that knocks your pool of candidates down from 12,500 to 2,500.
<v Ira Glass>然后当然还要考虑有多少人是你真正觉得有吸引力的。即使你给出一个很高的比例，比如五分之一，那也会把你候选人的池子从1.25万缩减到2500人。

00:03:37.090 --> 00:03:39.220
<v David Kestenbaum>In the whole city of Boston, right?
<v David Kestenbaum>在整个波士顿市？

00:03:39.220 --> 00:03:39.950
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>是的。

00:03:39.950 --> 00:03:42.670
<v David Kestenbaum>That's just like a needle in a haystack.
<v David Kestenbaum>那简直就像大海捞针。

00:03:42.670 --> 00:03:56.780
<v Ira Glass>And that 2,500 is before you get to anything personal, like your religion or how you see the world. What's your sense of humor? So David and his fellow students are talking about this, these rather kind of depressing numbers. And one of the professors comes in.
<v Ira Glass>而那2500人甚至还没算上任何个性化的因素，比如你的宗教信仰或世界观。你的幽默感如何？所以大卫和他的同学们就在讨论这个，这些相当令人沮丧的数字。这时一位教授走了进来。

00:03:56.780 --> 00:04:15.420
<v David Kestenbaum>She's not married, either. And so we start to draw it for her, and then we started to say, well OK, half of them are men, so we'd circle half. And then we'd say, well, what's the age group you're interested in? And then we'd sort of circle a smaller subset. And then she had all these other requirements, like the guy had to be taller than her. And she's pretty tall.
<v David Kestenbaum>她也没结婚。于是我们开始为她推算，然后我们说，好吧，假设一半是男性，我们就圈掉一半。然后说，好吧，你感兴趣的年龄段是多少？然后我们又圈掉一个更小的子集。接着她还有其他要求，比如男生必须比她高。而她个子挺高的。

00:04:15.420 --> 00:04:16.070
<v David Kestenbaum>[LAUGHTER]
<v David Kestenbaum>[笑声]

00:04:16.070 --> 00:04:25.120
<v David Kestenbaum>That really limited things. And then she said he had to be smarter than her. You know, and she's a Harvard physics professor, so that was even smaller. And basically, we got down to there being nobody.
<v David Kestenbaum>这真的限制很大。然后她说他必须比她聪明。你知道的，她是哈佛的物理学教授，所以符合条件的人就更少了。基本上，我们算到最后一个人都不剩。

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:29.720
<v David Kestenbaum>[LAUGHTER]
<v David Kestenbaum>[笑声]

00:04:29.720 --> 00:04:35.690
<v David Kestenbaum>She's alone.
<v David Kestenbaum>她孤身一人。

00:04:35.690 --> 00:04:43.520
<v Ira Glass>During this period of your life when you would think about these numbers, were you sure the entire time that there was somebody out there?
<v Ira Glass>在你思考这些数字的人生阶段里，你一直确定世界上有某个人存在吗？

00:04:43.520 --> 00:05:00.840
<v David Kestenbaum>Yeah. I don't know why. But you know, at the beginning of every mathematical proof, people often write, "Assume that there exists X." Assume we have an infinite surface bound by something, or whatever.
<v David Kestenbaum>是的。我不知道为什么。但你知道，在每个数学证明的开头，人们通常会写“假设存在X”。假设我们有一个被某种东西包围的无限平面，等等。

00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:01.250
<v Ira Glass>Right.
<v Ira Glass>没错。

00:05:01.250 --> 00:05:17.240
<v David Kestenbaum>It's like, assume there exists some girlfriend. There's totally that act of faith underneath it, yeah. But I had a more scientific view, which is that there are people out there who might be right for me, not just one person. Like that seemed like in a silly novel or something. You know.
<v David Kestenbaum>就像是，假设存在某个女朋友。那种信念之下完全是一种信心。但我有更科学的观点，那就是世界上有很多人可能适合我，不只是一个人。那种“唯一一个”的想法就像愚蠢小说里的情节。你知道的。

00:05:17.240 --> 00:05:17.630
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>是啊。

00:05:17.630 --> 00:05:23.340
<v David Kestenbaum>I don't believe there's just one. If there were just one person out there, good luck. They could speak Chinese, you know?
<v David Kestenbaum>我不相信只有一个。如果世界上真的只有一个人，祝你好运。她可能还会说中文呢，你知道吗？

00:05:23.340 --> 00:05:23.910
<v David Kestenbaum>[LAUGHTER]
<v David Kestenbaum>[笑声]

00:05:23.910 --> 00:05:32.420
<v David Kestenbaum>And they probably do, right? What are the odds you're going to find them and a translator? You got to believe there's more than one person.
<v David Kestenbaum>而且很可能就是这样，对吧？你找到她还要顺便找个翻译的概率是多少？你必须相信不止一个人。

00:05:32.420 --> 00:05:45.360
<v Ira Glass>But if you do believe that there is more than one person for you, you really might want to keep that belief to yourself sometimes. This may be one of those ideas that you don't want to take out of the classroom and bring into the real world. Case in point.
<v Ira Glass>但如果你真的相信有不止一个人适合你，有时你最好还是把这个信念藏在心里。这可能是那种你不该带出课堂、带进现实世界的想法。举个例子。

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:51.920
<v Alex Blumberg>It was definitely early on in our relationship. And I think it was our first big fight.
<v Alex Blumberg>那绝对是在我们关系的早期。我想那是我们的第一次大争吵。

00:05:51.920 --> 00:06:04.050
<v Ira Glass>This is somebody else from the immediate word of our radio show-- Alex Blumberg, one of our producers. A while ago, he and his then future wife, Nazanin, were out on a date. And because they were newly in love, the topic of conversation was--
<v Ira Glass>这是我们电台节目组里的另一个人——我们的制片人之一，亚历克斯·布伦伯格。不久前，他和当时的未婚妻纳扎宁正在约会。因为他们刚陷入热恋，谈话的主题是——

00:06:04.050 --> 00:06:13.870
<v Alex Blumberg>How great it was that we were in love. [LAUGHS] And how happy we were to have found each other. And it felt so fated, you know?
<v Alex Blumberg>我们相爱是多么美好。[笑] 以及我们找到彼此是多么幸运。而且感觉真是命中注定，你知道吗？

00:06:13.870 --> 00:06:36.930
<v Alex Blumberg>And she asked, do you really think that we were the only one for each other? And I said, I don't know if you're the only one for me, but I think that you have to be at least one in 100,000, is what I said. Which I thought was-- in retrospect, now that I'm telling this story, that sounds really bad.
<v Alex Blumberg>她问，你真的认为我们是彼此的唯一吗？我说，我不知道你是不是我的唯一，但我认为你至少是10万分之一，我是这么说的。回想起来，当我讲这个故事时，我觉得那听起来真的很糟糕。

00:06:36.930 --> 00:06:38.570
<v Alex Blumberg>[LAUGHTER]
<v Alex Blumberg>[笑声]

00:06:38.570 --> 00:06:42.190
<v Ira Glass>I was kind of holding my tongue over here, actually. Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>其实我在旁边一直在咬舌头强忍着。是啊。

00:06:42.190 --> 00:06:49.320
<v Alex Blumberg>At the time, I thought it was a romantic statement. Because one out of 100,000-- what is there, like 6 or 7 billion people on the planet?
<v Alex Blumberg>当时，我觉得那是一个浪漫的陈述。因为10万分之一——地球上有60亿还是70亿人？

00:06:49.320 --> 00:06:50.260
<v Ira Glass>Sure.
<v Ira Glass>当然。

00:06:50.260 --> 00:06:57.220
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>He picked 100,000, which probably doesn't make any scientific sense and also made me feel bad.
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>他选了10万分之一，这可能没有任何科学意义，而且也让我感觉很不好。

00:06:57.220 --> 00:07:08.340
<v Ira Glass>So if Alex thought that saying that there are 100,000 other women that he could love was simply another way of saying how rare love is-- 100,000 seemed like a small number to Alex. Nazanin did not see it that way.
<v Ira Glass>所以，如果亚历克斯认为说“还有10万个他可能爱上的女性”仅仅是表达爱是多么罕见的一种方式——10万对亚历克斯来说似乎是个小数目。纳扎宁可不这么看。

00:07:08.340 --> 00:07:21.960
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>I know it's ridiculous to think that there's one person out there for everybody, but it definitely feels that way, right? When you're, like, falling in love. And it's not like I actually expect him to believe it, but he wouldn't even say it. [LAUGHS]
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>我知道认为每个人都有一个“唯一”是很荒谬的，但那种感觉确实是那样的，对吧？当你坠入爱河的时候。并不是说我真的指望他相信，但他连说都不肯说。[笑]

00:07:21.960 --> 00:07:22.820
<v Ira Glass>Wait, he wouldn't say--
<v Ira Glass>等等，他不肯说——

00:07:22.820 --> 00:07:28.240
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>Like he wouldn't even just say I was the only person for him. Like he couldn't even--
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>就像他连说我是他唯一的爱人都做不到。他甚至无法——

00:07:28.240 --> 00:07:28.990
<v Ira Glass>He had to get all scientific.
<v Ira Glass>他非得搞得那么科学。

00:07:28.990 --> 00:07:34.590
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>Yeah, exactly. He had to get all scientific. Like he couldn't just lie for a second.
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>是的，没错。他非得搞得那么科学。就像他连撒个谎都不行。

00:07:34.590 --> 00:07:39.330
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>[LAUGHTER]
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>[笑声]

00:07:39.330 --> 00:08:06.490
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>It's just like a fundamental difference, you know? Like to me, it makes me feel good to think that we're the only ones out there for each other. And to him, I think it makes him feel bad. I think it freaks him out. I think the idea that, like, I'm it, in the whole world, makes him feel really-- I don't know. It just seems impossible and stupid, and not-- and also, just like, it's a lot of pressure.
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>这简直是一个根本性的分歧，你知道吗？对我来说，认为我们是彼此的唯一让我感觉很好。而对他来说，我想这让他感觉很糟。这让他抓狂。我认为“我是他的唯一，在这个世界上”这个想法让他感觉真的很……我不知道。这看起来似乎不可能且愚蠢，而且……还有，这压力太大了。

00:08:06.490 --> 00:08:14.440
<v Ira Glass>Yeah. It's a lot of pressure. There's a saying that goes something like, "How terrible to love what can perish." You know?
<v Ira Glass>是啊。这压力太大了。有句谚语大概是说：“爱上会消逝的东西是多么可怕。”你知道吗？

00:08:14.440 --> 00:08:28.740
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>Right. Right. Exactly. But like, you know, there's like 100,000 people. It's not that terrible to love something that can perish. [LAUGHS]
<v Nazanin Rafsanjani>对。对。正是如此。但就像，你知道，有10万人呢。爱上会消逝的东西并没有那么可怕。[笑]

00:08:28.740 --> 00:08:55.510
<v Ira Glass>Well, from WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life, distributed by Public Radio International. Today on our show, stories of people looking for and finding the one person out there who is just for them, even if it is just a pleasant lie that there is just one person out there for each of us. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>这里是芝加哥WBEZ电台，《美国生活》节目，由国际公共广播电台发行。今天在我们的节目中，讲述那些寻找并找到命中注定之人的故事，即使“每个人都有唯一一人”只是一个美好的谎言。请继续收听。

00:08:55.510 --> 00:09:12.890
<v Ira Glass>Act One, "It's Not Over Till the Fat Man Sings." What's amazing about this guy who you're about to hear next is that he kind of decides that this girl is for him, and he pursues this girl against some really tough odds, even before he's fully in love with the girl. Sarah Koenig tells what happened.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，“不到胖子歌唱完就不算结束”。你接下来要听到的这个家伙，最令人惊奇的是，他认定那个女孩就是他的真命天女，并且他在胜算极其渺茫的情况下追求她，甚至在那时还没完全爱上她。莎拉·柯尼格讲述了发生的一切。

00:09:12.890 --> 00:09:22.630
<v Sarah Koenig>This romance begins with a man named Bao Gong, a strong, masculine man. An incorruptible judge.
<v Sarah Koenig>这段罗曼史始于一个名叫包公的男人，一个强壮、阳刚的男人。一位铁面无私的法官。

00:09:22.630 --> 00:09:33.800
<v Ira Glass>Bao Gong was a famous character in Beijing Opera. And In 1995, he was played, briefly, by a 6'3" 250-pound white guy from the Midwest named Eric Hayot.
<v Ira Glass>包公是京剧中的著名角色。1995年，这个角色由一位来自中西部、身高6英尺3英寸（约1.9米）、体重250磅（约113公斤）的白人小伙埃里克·海约特短暂地扮演过。

00:09:33.800 --> 00:09:41.250
<v Eric Hayot>And then there was the really-- there was this fast part where he's reading the scroll. It was like-- [SINGING]
<v Eric Hayot>还有那段非常……那段他在读圣旨的快板部分。就像是——[唱歌]

00:09:41.250 --> 00:09:46.800
<v Eric Hayot>Oh, now I'm not going to remember the rest. I don't know where it goes. I thought I could handle it. But it's something like that.
